## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates understanding of fixed-size memory allocators, O(1) operations, and basic safety guarantees in constrained memory environments—core knowledge for systems programming and embedded development.

## Key Concepts

- **Fixed-size block allocator**: Divides a pre-allocated pool into uniform blocks, eliminating external fragmentation
- **In-block free list**: Stores next-pointer directly within each free block, using only 8 bytes per block
- **O(1) operations**: Both allocate and free simply manipulate a head pointer—no traversal required
- **8-byte alignment**: Block size is naturally aligned; allocator returns properly aligned addresses
- **Double-free protection**: Tracks block state via flags or list membership checks
- **Fragmentation testing**: Measure allocation success rates under various patterns; track internal fragmentation (fixed by design, none) and external fragmentation (none for fixed-size)
- **Memory safety testing**: Validate bounds, check for leaks via allocation/free counts, fuzz with random operations

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stddef.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

#define POOL_SIZE (256 * 1024)    // 256 KiB
#define BLOCK_SIZE 128            // 8-byte aligned
#define NUM_BLOCKS (POOL_SIZE / BLOCK_SIZE)

typedef struct {
    uint32_t next;                // Index of next free block
} free_block_t;

static uint8_t pool[POOL_SIZE];
static uint32_t free_head = 0;    // Head of free list
static bool allocated[NUM_BLOCKS]; // Track block state

void init_pool(void) {
    free_head = 0;
    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < NUM_BLOCKS; i++) {
        free_block_t* block = (free_block_t*)(pool + i * BLOCK_SIZE);
        block->next = (i + 1 < NUM_BLOCKS) ? i + 1 : 0xFFFFFFFF;
        allocated[i] = false;
    }
}

void* allocate(void) {
    if (free_head == 0xFFFFFFFF) return NULL;

    uint32_t index = free_head;
    free_block_t* block = (free_block_t*)(pool + index * BLOCK_SIZE);
    free_head = block->next;
    allocated[index] = true;

    return (void*)block;
}

bool free(void* ptr) {
    if (ptr == NULL || ptr < pool || ptr >= pool + POOL_SIZE) return false;

    uint32_t index = ((uint8_t*)ptr - pool) / BLOCK_SIZE;
    if (index >= NUM_BLOCKS || !allocated[index]) return false; // Double-free protection

    allocated[index] = false;
    free_block_t* block = (free_block_t*)(pool + index * BLOCK_SIZE);
    block->next = free_head;
    free_head = index;

    return true;
}
```

## Testing Strategy

**Fragmentation**: With fixed-size blocks, external fragmentation is impossible. Test by repeatedly allocating and freeing in random patterns; all allocations should succeed until the pool is exhausted.

**Memory Safety**:
- Verify bounds on allocate/free calls
- Test double-free detection returns false
- Test free of invalid pointers returns false
- Run with address sanitizer to catch overflows
- Track allocation count vs. free count to detect leaks

## Why This Is Asked
Tests basic concurrent data structure design and practical use of atomics and memory ordering in a beginner-friendly but realistic setting.

## Key Concepts
- MPSC synchronization using a shared tail and a local consumer head
- Atomic fetch_add for slot reservation
- Memory ordering: release on enqueue, acquire on dequeue
- Correct wrap-around handling in a fixed-size buffer

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-C code (C11 atomics style)
#define N (1<<12)
typedef struct { uint64_t data; uint64_t seq; } slot_t;
slot_t buf[N];
atomic_uintptr_t tail = 0; // producer index
size_t head = 0; // consumer index

bool enqueue(uint64_t v){
  size_t t = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  if (t >= N) return false;
  buf[t].data = v;
  atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
  buf[t].seq = t; // simple wrap-safe signal
  return true;
}

bool dequeue(uint64_t* out){
  if (head >= atomic_load_explicit(&tail, memory_order_acquire)) return false;
  *out = buf[head].data;
  head++;
  return true;
}
```

